add_runs(p116, "If children and adults show similar association effects, children are sensitive to action–result association in the same way as adults, and their stricter judgments of V1 descriptions hold regardless of how strongly the action is associated with the result. Their lower acceptance would then reflect a general difference in how children evaluate V1 descriptions of failed attempts. Three explanations of this difference cannot be separated by the binary task: Children may find it harder than adults to set aside an inference that the result occurred, may reject a true description as inadequate because it leaves an expected result unaddressed, or may judge a failed attempt to be a poor instance of the action. If, instead, the association effect is larger in children, their stricter judgments would be located mainly in V1s that name strongly associated actions; the pragmatic explanation would fit this pattern less naturally, because children tend to be more tolerant than adults of underinformative descriptions in binary judgment tasks (Katsos & Bishop, 2011). Because adult acceptance in zero-result events may approach ceiling, however, a pattern found in acceptance probabilities need not hold on the log-odds scale, and conclusions take both scales into account.")
